  2. README.md, section "License", lines 299 to 301:
     https://github.com/adilkairolla/macpaper-engine/blob/5e316a7edcf79e6472920718c732b8d8bcfa541f/README.md

       "## License

       [MIT](LICENSE) © Adil Kairolla"

The README also says, for the sample wallpapers: "Like everything here, they're MIT-licensed."
(README.md, line 151), and "No third-party dependencies." (line 286). The repository contains
no other licence or notice file.

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper; see the note at the top of
main.metal. What was changed from the original:

  - ocean.metal and film-grain.metal were merged into one mainImage function. The MacPaper
    entry point and its Pixel, Frame, Params and Inputs types are gone; frame.time is iTime and
    the canvas aspect is the size of the surface being drawn.
  - The picture is flipped from the original's top-left origin to our bottom-left fragCoord, so
    it is the same way up.
  - The engine helpers (mp_hash, mp_noise, mp_fbm, mp_luma, mp_rotate) are copied in unchanged.
  - The exposed parameters are constants at their default values (sun, horizon, speed, waves,
    scale and the "Golden" colours); the sun is no longer draggable.
  - Two smoothstep calls with their edges in descending order are written as one minus the
    ascending form (the same curve, but defined in Metal).
  - The ocean colour is clamped to 0..1 before the grain, as the original's 8-bit intermediate
    target did.
  - The film grain is redrawn 4 times a second instead of 24, so it does not shimmer on a
    desktop. Its strength and cell size are unchanged.
  - Nothing else: the waves, sun, clouds, colours and their speeds are the author's.

